# Copy to .env and fill in from the Firebase console (Project settings > Your apps),
# or derive from android/app/google-services.json.

VITE_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
VITE_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
VITE_FIREBASE_DATABASE_URL=
VITE_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_STORAGE_BUCKET=
VITE_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
VITE_FIREBASE_APP_ID=

# ── Ads ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Three switches, and they do different jobs. Confusing them is how you either
# ship with no ads or click your own live ones.
#
#   VITE_ADS_PROVIDER            'admob' runs the real SDK · 'fake' draws a placeholder
#   VITE_ADMOB_TESTING           'true' makes the SDK return TEST ads whatever unit
#                                id you give it — no revenue, no impressions on a
#                                live unit, safe to tap. All or nothing: every user
#                                gets test ads too, so revenue is zero.
#   VITE_ADMOB_TEST_DEVICE_IDS   the narrow version of the same protection. Only
#                                the listed devices get test ads; everyone else
#                                gets real ones and earns. This is what lets you
#                                keep testing after going live.
#
# Google publishes fixed sample unit ids that always return a test ad. Develop
# against those from the start; waiting until release means shipping consent
# and lifecycle code that has never run.
#
#   ca-app-pub-3940256099942544/6300978111   banner
#   ca-app-pub-3940256099942544/1033173712   interstitial
#   ca-app-pub-3940256099942544/5224354917   rewarded
#   ca-app-pub-3940256099942544~3347511713   app id (manifest)
#
# Going live, in this order:
#   1. npm i @capacitor-community/admob && npx cap sync
#   2. AdMob console → add the app → create the three ad units
#   3. put the real unit ids below, and the real app id in
#      android/app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml (APPLICATION_ID — a tilde, not a slash)
#   4. publish app-ads.txt at the developer website declared in the Play listing.
#      Required for new AdMob apps since January 2025; without it a new app does
#      not serve at full fill. One line:
#          google.com, pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX, DIRECT
#   5. keep VITE_ADMOB_TESTING=true and check on a device that ads still load.
#      A typo in a unit id fails silently: no ad, no error, no revenue.
#   6. register your own device below, THEN set VITE_ADMOB_TESTING=false.
#      Doing 6 in that order matters — see the warning.
#
# ⚠️ "Do not tap ads in your own build" does not mean "do not install it". You
#    have to run the app on a real device; that is the only way to find out
#    whether the banner is where you think it is. What you must not do is tap a
#    LIVE ad, and the fix Google provides is to register the device rather than
#    to stop testing.
#
#    Run the app once and read the id out of logcat:
#        adb logcat | findstr "RequestConfiguration.Builder"
#    Paste the hash below. Registered devices then receive test ads — labelled
#    "Test Ad", safe to tap, no effect on reports — while every other device
#    gets real ads. The id differs per device and can change on reinstall.
#
#    Leave it empty and VITE_ADMOB_TESTING is the only protection you have, so
#    fill it in BEFORE turning that off, not after.
VITE_ADS_PROVIDER=fake
VITE_ADMOB_TESTING=true
VITE_ADMOB_TEST_DEVICE_IDS=
VITE_ADMOB_BANNER_ID=ca-app-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/XXXXXXXXXX
VITE_ADMOB_INTERSTITIAL_ID=ca-app-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/XXXXXXXXXX
VITE_ADMOB_REWARDED_ID=ca-app-pub-XXXXXXXXXXXXXXXX/XXXXXXXXXX

# ── Billing ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 'none' (default) means nothing can be charged, so the Remove Ads entry point
# and screen are both hidden. Keep it on until the product is live: a purchase
# flow that cannot take money hands the product over free and tells the player
# they bought it.
#
# ⚠️ The steps that used to be written here were wrong in three ways, and I
#    followed them and gave the wrong sequence twice. Corrected 2026-09-11:
#
#   1. The plugin has to come FIRST, not the product. Play Console will not
#      create a one-time product until an uploaded build declares
#      com.android.vending.BILLING, and that permission arrives with the Play
#      Billing Library the plugin pulls in. The console shows an upload button
#      where the create flow should be.
#
#   2. @capacitor-community/in-app-purchases does not exist — npm returns 404.
#      Real options are @capgo/native-purchases (direct, free, its major
#      version tracks Capacitor), @revenuecat/purchases-capacitor (free to
#      $2,500 monthly tracked revenue, then 1% of gross) and
#      cordova-plugin-purchase. None has been adopted yet.
#
#   3. "No code changes" was false. src/billing/playBillingProvider.js was
#      written against an API that does not exist: every method name, argument
#      name, return field and the plugin registration name differ from the real
#      plugins. It needs rewriting, not enabling.
#
# Google also renamed 인앱 상품 to 일회성 제품, and a product now needs at least
# one purchase option. An un-activated purchase option stays 임시 and the
# Billing Library does not return it — no error, just a product that appears
# not to exist.
#
# See docs/BACKLOG.md R-5 for the full sequence. It needs two uploads, and
# VITE_BILLING_PROVIDER=play has to be the later one: isAvailable only asks
# whether the plugin is present, never whether the product exists, so flipping
# it early gives the player a buy button with no price that errors when tapped.
VITE_BILLING_PROVIDER=none
